From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: macbox <macbox@localhost>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:09:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] Coherent: a CPU write through a surface's own mapping
 tells the traps (R1)

- write_surface_bytes (replaceRegion: into a surface made GPU-read-only, row
  by row into the guest's pages) wrote under a trapping surface with no
  notice, so the image another texture over the same pages held stayed stale
  and its later copy back could overwrite the write. It now tells
  shadow_coherent::host_writes of the view of the bytes and of each run of
  pages they land in, before they land; the os::surface lend test checks the
  ranges and the order.
- The IOSurface ring's test pattern write (IoSurfaceTable::test_pattern) does
  the same through a new Surface::host_writes (no-op by default, the Linux
  surface's is surface_device_access).
- host_meets' doc comment sat on surface_device_access.
- vendor/qemu 82cef5aac4: address_space_ld*/st* reach the census.

Not hooked, as they cannot meet a surface's pages: GuestPtr writes and reads
(FIFO rings, device and display registers, task roots and object lists,
placement words, the IOSurface descriptor table, the ICB write-back into its
own guest buffer) are the guest kernel's protocol memory; the display's
property buffers and rate-map samples are the library's own memory; Map::write
and WritableCursor have no product caller over surface pages. Reims tests
single-threaded, before and after: vulkan lib 410 passed 21 failed, core lib
2376 passed 1 failed, the same failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
